    {"id":"hq-ah-two-sum","problemId":"lc-1-two-sum","topic":"Arrays & Hashing","topicId":"arrays-hashing","subtopic":"Frequency Map","subtopicId":"frequency-map","difficulty":"easy","title":"Two Sum — O(n)","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Two Sum (LC 1): Given nums and target, return indices of two numbers that sum to target. Which achieves O(n)?","answer":"HashMap storing num→index; check if (target - num) is in map at each step","acceptedAnswers":["hashmap","num→index","use a hashmap"],"choices":["HashMap storing num→index; check if (target - num) is in map at each step","Sort array, use two pointers from each end","Binary search for each complement","Nested loop checking every pair"],"tags":["hashmap","recognition"],"timeLimitSec":15,"masteryWeight":1.0,"fullCode":"def twoSum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}\n    for i, n in enumerate(nums):\n        if target - n in seen:\n            return [seen[target - n], i]\n        seen[n] = i","fullExplanation":"Use hashmap for O(1) complement lookup. Store num→index. At each element check if (target - current) exists. Sorting+two-pointers works but loses original indices.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/two-sum/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-bs-rotated-min","problemId":"lc-153-find-min-rotated","topic":"Binary Search","topicId":"binary-search","subtopic":"Rotated Array","subtopicId":"rotated-array","difficulty":"medium","title":"Find Min in Rotated — decision","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array (LC 153): When nums[mid] > nums[right], where is the minimum?","answer":"RIGHT half — set left = mid + 1","acceptedAnswers":["right half","left = mid + 1"],"choices":["RIGHT half — set left = mid + 1","LEFT half — set right = mid - 1","At nums[mid] — return immediately","Cannot determine — check both halves"],"tags":["binary-search","rotated"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.3,"fullCode":"def findMin(nums):\n    l, r = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while l < r:\n        mid = (l + r) // 2\n        if nums[mid] > nums[r]: l = mid + 1  # min on RIGHT\n        else: r = mid\n    return nums[l]","fullExplanation":"If nums[mid] > nums[right], the rotation jump is in the right portion → minimum is in the RIGHT half. If nums[mid] <= nums[right], minimum is at mid or left. Always compare against nums[right].","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-bs-left-bound","problemId":"","topic":"Binary Search","topicId":"binary-search","subtopic":"Left Bound","subtopicId":"left-bound","difficulty":"medium","title":"Binary Search left bound","questionType":"fill_blank","prompt":"Binary search LEFT bound (first occurrence): when nums[mid] == target, set r = ___ (not mid-1).","answer":"r = mid","acceptedAnswers":["r = mid","right = mid","hi = mid"],"choices":[],"tags":["binary-search","boundary"],"timeLimitSec":12,"masteryWeight":1.0,"fullCode":"def left_bound(nums, target):\n    l, r = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while l < r:\n        mid = (l + r) // 2\n        if nums[mid] < target: l = mid + 1\n        else: r = mid  # keep mid\n    return l if nums[l] == target else -1","fullExplanation":"Left bound: r=mid keeps mid in range when match found. Right bound: l=mid; use upper mid=(l+r+1)//2 to avoid infinite loop.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-sw-non-neg","problemId":"","topic":"Sliding Window","topicId":"sliding-window","subtopic":"Variable Size","subtopicId":"variable-size","difficulty":"medium","title":"Sliding Window — non-negative rule","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"A shrinkable sliding window only works correctly when all values are ___. Why?","answer":"Non-negative — adding always increases sum, shrinking always decreases (monotone property needed)","acceptedAnswers":["non-negative","positive or zero"],"choices":["Non-negative — adding always increases sum, shrinking always decreases (monotone property needed)","Positive integers only — zeros cause stall","Any integers — window auto-adjusts","Sorted — sliding window requires sorted input"],"tags":["sliding-window","pitfall"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.3,"fullCode":"def maxLen(nums, k):\n    l = total = res = 0\n    for r in range(len(nums)):\n        total += nums[r]\n        while total > k: total -= nums[l]; l += 1\n        res = max(res, r - l + 1)\n    return res","fullExplanation":"With negatives: removing from left might INCREASE sum — breaks shrinking logic. The window assumes monotone relationship. With negatives → prefix sum + hashmap instead.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-sw-vs-prefix","problemId":"lc-560-subarray-sum-k","topic":"Sliding Window","topicId":"sliding-window","subtopic":"Contrast","subtopicId":"contrast","difficulty":"medium","title":"Subarray Sum = k — SW vs Prefix","questionType":"this_or_that","prompt":"Subarray Sum Equals K (LC 560): array has NEGATIVE integers, find subarrays with sum = k. Sliding window or prefix sum?","answer":"Prefix Sum + Hashmap — sliding window fails with negatives. prefix[j]-prefix[i]=k → count (prefix-k) seen before.","acceptedAnswers":["prefix sum","prefix sum + hashmap"],"choices":["Prefix Sum + Hashmap — sliding window fails with negatives. prefix[j]-prefix[i]=k → count (prefix-k) seen before.","Sliding Window — works for all values","Both equivalent — choose by preference","Binary search on cumulative sum"],"tags":["contrast","prefix-sum","sliding-window"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.4,"fullCode":"from collections import defaultdict\ndef subarraySum(nums, k):\n    prefix_count = defaultdict(int)\n    prefix_count[0] = 1\n    prefix = res = 0\n    for n in nums:\n        prefix += n\n        res += prefix_count[prefix - k]\n        prefix_count[prefix] += 1\n    return res","fullExplanation":"KEY: sliding window needs non-negative. Negatives → prefix sum + hashmap. If prefix[j]-prefix[i]=k, subarray [i+1..j] sums to k. Store prefix_count[prefix] = times seen.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/subarray-sum-equals-k/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-ms-direction","problemId":"","topic":"Stack / Monotonic Stack","topicId":"stack-monotonic-stack","subtopic":"Monotonic Stack","subtopicId":"monotonic-stack","difficulty":"medium","title":"Monotonic Stack direction","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"To find NEXT GREATER element: maintain ___ stack. To find NEXT SMALLER: maintain ___ stack.","answer":"Decreasing for next greater; Increasing for next smaller","acceptedAnswers":["decreasing for next greater","decreasing stack"],"choices":["Decreasing for next greater; Increasing for next smaller","Increasing for next greater; Decreasing for next smaller","Both use decreasing; comparison operator changes","Direction doesn't matter — pop condition controls it"],"tags":["monotonic-stack","direction"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.3,"fullCode":"def nextGreater(nums):\n    res = [-1]*len(nums); stack = []\n    for i, n in enumerate(nums):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] < n:\n            res[stack.pop()] = n\n        stack.append(i)\n    return res","fullExplanation":"DECREASING stack: pop when current is GREATER → gives NEXT GREATER. INCREASING stack: pop when current is SMALLER → gives NEXT SMALLER. Always store INDICES.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-ps-balance","problemId":"lc-525-contiguous-array","topic":"Prefix Sum","topicId":"prefix-sum","subtopic":"Balance Key","subtopicId":"balance-key","difficulty":"medium","title":"Contiguous Array — 0→-1 trick","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Contiguous Array (LC 525): Longest subarray with equal 0s and 1s. What transformation enables prefix sum?","answer":"Replace 0 with -1; find longest subarray sum=0 using prefix balance hashmap","acceptedAnswers":["replace 0 with -1","0→-1","map 0 to -1"],"choices":["Replace 0 with -1; find longest subarray sum=0 using prefix balance hashmap","Track (count_0, count_1) tuples as key","Binary search on subarray length","Sort to group 0s and 1s, then two pointers"],"tags":["prefix-sum","balance","transformation"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.3,"fullCode":"def findMaxLength(nums):\n    prefix_index = {0: -1}\n    balance = res = 0\n    for i, n in enumerate(nums):\n        balance += 1 if n==1 else -1\n        if balance in prefix_index: res = max(res, i - prefix_index[balance])\n        else: prefix_index[balance] = i  # earliest index\n    return res","fullExplanation":"Transform 0→-1. Equal 0s and 1s → sum=0. Same balance at i and j → subarray [i+1..j] sums to 0. Store EARLIEST index for each balance. Only store if balance not in map.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/contiguous-array/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-tr-diameter","problemId":"lc-543-diameter","topic":"Trees","topicId":"trees","subtopic":"Path Problems","subtopicId":"path-problems","difficulty":"easy","title":"Tree Diameter — return vs track","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Diameter of Binary Tree (LC 543): at each node, what do you RETURN to parent vs what do you TRACK globally?","answer":"Return 1+max(l,r) (height); Track max(l+r) globally (diameter through this node)","acceptedAnswers":["return height track diameter","1+max(l,r)","return height"],"choices":["Return 1+max(l,r) (height); Track max(l+r) globally (diameter through this node)","Return l+r (diameter); Track 1+max(l,r) globally","Return max(l,r); Track l+r+1","Return and track same value — equals height"],"tags":["trees","dfs","diameter"],"timeLimitSec":18,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"def diameterOfBinaryTree(root):\n    res = [0]\n    def depth(node):\n        if not node: return 0\n        l = depth(node.left); r = depth(node.right)\n        res[0] = max(res[0], l + r)\n        return 1 + max(l, r)\n    depth(root); return res[0]","fullExplanation":"Separate RETURN (height=1+max(l,r)) from TRACK (diameter=l+r). Common trap: returning l+r breaks height computation for parent.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/diameter-of-binary-tree/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-tr-level-order","problemId":"lc-102-level-order","topic":"Trees","topicId":"trees","subtopic":"BFS Level Order","subtopicId":"bfs-level-order","difficulty":"medium","title":"Level Order BFS template","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Binary Tree Level Order (LC 102): the key that processes exactly ONE level per iteration is ___","answer":"Snapshot len(queue) at start of while loop: for _ in range(len(queue)) = exactly this level","acceptedAnswers":["snapshot queue length","range(len(queue))"],"choices":["Snapshot len(queue) at start of while loop: for _ in range(len(queue)) = exactly this level","None sentinel between levels","DFS with depth parameter","Pop until empty — one pop = one level"],"tags":["trees","bfs","level-order"],"timeLimitSec":15,"masteryWeight":1.1,"fullCode":"from collections import deque\ndef levelOrder(root):\n    if not root: return []\n    queue = deque([root]); res = []\n    while queue:\n        level = []\n        for _ in range(len(queue)):  # snapshot = this level\n            node = queue.popleft(); level.append(node.val)\n            if node.left: queue.append(node.left)\n            if node.right: queue.append(node.right)\n        res.append(level)\n    return res","fullExplanation":"Template: snapshot len(queue) = nodes in current level. Process that many nodes. Their children = next level. Canonical BFS level-order pattern.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/binary-tree-level-order-traversal/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-bt-combo-vs-perm","problemId":"","topic":"Backtracking","topicId":"backtracking","subtopic":"Combinations vs Permutations","subtopicId":"contrast","difficulty":"medium","title":"Combinations vs Permutations","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Backtracking — key structural difference between combinations and permutations:","answer":"Combinations: loop from 'start'; Permutations: loop from 0 with visited set","acceptedAnswers":["loop from start vs 0","combinations from start"],"choices":["Combinations: loop from 'start'; Permutations: loop from 0 with visited set","Both loop from 0; difference is duplicate handling","Combinations use BFS; Permutations use DFS","Permutations loop from start; Combinations loop from 0"],"tags":["backtracking","contrast"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.4,"fullCode":"def combine(n,k):\n    res=[]\n    def bt(start, path):\n        if len(path)==k: res.append(path[:]); return\n        for i in range(start,n+1): bt(i+1, path+[i])\n    bt(1,[]); return res\n\ndef permute(nums):\n    res=[]\n    def bt(path, used):\n        if len(path)==len(nums): res.append(path[:]); return\n        for i in range(len(nums)):\n            if not used[i]: used[i]=True; bt(path+[nums[i]],used); used[i]=False\n    bt([],[False]*len(nums)); return res","fullExplanation":"COMBINATIONS: pass start → each element used once, no order duplicates. PERMUTATIONS: always loop from 0 + visited set → every element at every position.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/combinations/","https://leetcode.com/problems/permutations/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-bt-subsets","problemId":"","topic":"Backtracking","topicId":"backtracking","subtopic":"Subsets","subtopicId":"subsets","difficulty":"medium","title":"Subsets vs Combination Sum — when to record","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Subsets (LC 78) vs Combination Sum (LC 39): when do you add to results?","answer":"Subsets: record at EVERY recursive call; Combination Sum: record only when remain==0","acceptedAnswers":["record every call vs remain 0","subsets always record"],"choices":["Subsets: record at EVERY recursive call; Combination Sum: record only when remain==0","Both record only at leaf nodes","Combination Sum records every call; Subsets only at target size","Both identical except duplicate handling"],"tags":["backtracking","subsets","contrast"],"timeLimitSec":18,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"def subsets(nums):\n    res=[]\n    def bt(start,path):\n        res.append(path[:])  # always record\n        for i in range(start,len(nums)): bt(i+1,path+[nums[i]])\n    bt(0,[]); return res\n\ndef combinationSum(c,target):\n    res=[]\n    def bt(start,remain,path):\n        if remain==0: res.append(path[:]); return\n        if remain<0: return\n        for i in range(start,len(c)): bt(i,remain-c[i],path+[c[i]])\n    bt(0,target,[]); return res","fullExplanation":"Subsets: add every intermediate path. CombSum: add only when remain==0. CombSum allows reuse (bt(i,...) not bt(i+1,...)).","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/subsets/","https://leetcode.com/problems/combination-sum/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
