    {"id":"hq-gr-bfs-unweighted","problemId":"","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"BFS vs DFS","subtopicId":"bfs-vs-dfs","difficulty":"easy","title":"BFS vs DFS — shortest path","questionType":"this_or_that","prompt":"Shortest path in UNWEIGHTED graph: BFS or DFS?","answer":"BFS — level-by-level; first time you reach destination = shortest path. DFS may find longer path first.","acceptedAnswers":["BFS","bfs"],"choices":["BFS — level-by-level; first time you reach destination = shortest path. DFS may find longer path first.","DFS — goes deep faster","Both produce same result","Dijkstra — always required for shortest path"],"tags":["graphs","bfs","shortest-path"],"timeLimitSec":12,"masteryWeight":1.0,"fullCode":"from collections import deque\ndef shortestPath(graph,src,dst):\n    q=deque([(src,0)]); vis={src}\n    while q:\n        node,d=q.popleft()\n        if node==dst: return d\n        for nei in graph[node]:\n            if nei not in vis: vis.add(nei); q.append((nei,d+1))\n    return -1","fullExplanation":"BFS for shortest path in unweighted graph. First arrival = min hops. Dijkstra for weighted. DFS finds A path, not necessarily shortest.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-gr-dijkstra","problemId":"","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"Shortest Path Algorithms","subtopicId":"shortest-path","difficulty":"medium","title":"BFS vs Dijkstra — when","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"When do you use Dijkstra instead of BFS?","answer":"When edge weights are non-uniform — BFS requires equal edge weights","acceptedAnswers":["non-uniform weights","weighted edges"],"choices":["When edge weights are non-uniform — BFS requires equal edge weights","When graph has cycles","When graph is directed","When graph has more than 1000 nodes"],"tags":["graphs","dijkstra","contrast"],"timeLimitSec":18,"masteryWeight":1.3,"fullCode":"import heapq\ndef dijkstra(graph,src,n):\n    dist=[float('inf')]*n; dist[src]=0\n    heap=[(0,src)]\n    while heap:\n        cost,u=heapq.heappop(heap)\n        if cost>dist[u]: continue\n        for v,w in graph[u]:\n            if dist[u]+w<dist[v]:\n                dist[v]=dist[u]+w; heapq.heappush(heap,(dist[v],v))\n    return dist","fullExplanation":"BFS: unit-weight graphs. Dijkstra: non-negative weighted. Bellman-Ford: negative edges. Dijkstra uses min-heap to always expand cheapest node.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-gr-pacific","problemId":"lc-417-pacific-atlantic","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"Matrix as Graph","subtopicId":"matrix-as-graph","difficulty":"medium","title":"Pacific Atlantic — reverse DFS","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Pacific Atlantic (LC 417): Why DFS from ocean BORDERS inward instead of from each cell outward?","answer":"O(mn) vs O(m²n²) — reverse DFS from borders visits each cell at most twice total","acceptedAnswers":["O(mn)","reverse reduces complexity","each cell once"],"choices":["O(mn) vs O(m²n²) — reverse DFS from borders visits each cell at most twice total","Simpler implementation","Border cells drain to both oceans","Oceans have no height — avoids edge cases"],"tags":["graphs","matrix","reverse-thinking"],"timeLimitSec":25,"masteryWeight":1.4,"fullCode":"def pacificAtlantic(heights):\n    m,n=len(heights),len(heights[0])\n    pac,atl=set(),set()\n    def dfs(r,c,vis):\n        vis.add((r,c))\n        for dr,dc in [(0,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(-1,0)]:\n            nr,nc=r+dr,c+dc\n            if 0<=nr<m and 0<=nc<n and (nr,nc) not in vis:\n                if heights[nr][nc]>=heights[r][c]: dfs(nr,nc,vis)\n    for r in range(m): dfs(r,0,pac); dfs(r,n-1,atl)\n    for c in range(n): dfs(0,c,pac); dfs(m-1,c,atl)\n    return [[r,c] for r in range(m) for c in range(n) if (r,c) in pac and (r,c) in atl]","fullExplanation":"Reverse: start from ocean borders and go UPHILL (neighbor height >= current). Each ocean does one DFS pass. Intersection = answer. Forward (each cell downhill) = O(m²n²).","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/pacific-atlantic-water-flow/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-gr-topo","problemId":"lc-210-course-schedule","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"Topological Sort","subtopicId":"topological-sort","difficulty":"medium","title":"Kahn's Topo — init queue","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Kahn's BFS topological sort (LC 210): initialize BFS queue with ___","answer":"All nodes with in-degree = 0 (no prerequisites)","acceptedAnswers":["in-degree 0","zero in-degree"],"choices":["All nodes with in-degree = 0 (no prerequisites)","Node 0 only","All nodes — filter as you process","Nodes with highest out-degree"],"tags":["topological-sort","kahn","in-degree"],"timeLimitSec":15,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"from collections import deque,defaultdict\ndef findOrder(n,prereqs):\n    g=defaultdict(list); ind=[0]*n\n    for a,b in prereqs: g[b].append(a); ind[a]+=1\n    q=deque([i for i in range(n) if ind[i]==0]); order=[]\n    while q:\n        node=q.popleft(); order.append(node)\n        for nei in g[node]:\n            ind[nei]-=1\n            if ind[nei]==0: q.append(nei)\n    return order if len(order)==n else []","fullExplanation":"Kahn's: 1) Build graph + in-degree. 2) Queue all in-degree-0 nodes. 3) Pop→order→decrement neighbors→enqueue if in-degree hits 0. 4) len(order)!=n → cycle.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule-ii/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-gr-union-find","problemId":"","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"Union Find","subtopicId":"union-find","difficulty":"medium","title":"Union Find — path compression","questionType":"fill_blank","prompt":"Union Find path compression: in find(x), set parent[x] = ___ to flatten tree.","answer":"find(parent[x])","acceptedAnswers":["find(parent[x])","root","recursive path compression"],"choices":[],"tags":["union-find","path-compression"],"timeLimitSec":15,"masteryWeight":1.0,"fullCode":"class UF:\n    def __init__(self,n): self.p=list(range(n)); self.r=[0]*n\n    def find(self,x):\n        if self.p[x]!=x: self.p[x]=self.find(self.p[x])  # path compression\n        return self.p[x]\n    def union(self,x,y):\n        rx,ry=self.find(x),self.find(y)\n        if rx==ry: return False\n        if self.r[rx]<self.r[ry]: rx,ry=ry,rx\n        self.p[ry]=rx\n        if self.r[rx]==self.r[ry]: self.r[rx]+=1\n        return True","fullExplanation":"Path compression: during find(), set parent[x]=root for every node on the path. Future finds are O(1). With union by rank → near O(α(n)) amortized.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-dp-knapsack-dir","problemId":"","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Knapsack DP","subtopicId":"knapsack-dp","difficulty":"medium","title":"0/1 Knapsack — loop direction","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Optimized 1D 0/1 Knapsack: iterate capacity from ___ to ___. Why?","answer":"RIGHT TO LEFT (high→0): prevents item being used twice in same pass","acceptedAnswers":["right to left","high to low","capacity down to 0"],"choices":["RIGHT TO LEFT (high→0): prevents item being used twice in same pass","LEFT TO RIGHT — ensures all capacities considered","Either direction — same result","LEFT TO RIGHT for 0/1, RIGHT TO LEFT for unbounded (opposite of truth)"],"tags":["dp","knapsack","loop-direction"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.5,"fullCode":"def knapsack01(weights,values,cap):\n    dp=[0]*(cap+1)\n    for i in range(len(weights)):\n        for w in range(cap,weights[i]-1,-1):  # RIGHT TO LEFT\n            dp[w]=max(dp[w],dp[w-weights[i]]+values[i])\n    return dp[cap]\n\ndef unbounded(weights,values,cap):\n    dp=[0]*(cap+1)\n    for i in range(len(weights)):\n        for w in range(weights[i],cap+1):  # LEFT TO RIGHT = allow reuse\n            dp[w]=max(dp[w],dp[w-weights[i]]+values[i])\n    return dp[cap]","fullExplanation":"0/1 → RIGHT TO LEFT: dp[w-weight] = previous item's row (not yet updated this pass). L→R: dp[w-weight] already includes item i → item used twice = unbounded. UNBOUNDED → intentionally L→R to allow reuse.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-dp-coin-vs-comb4","problemId":"","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Knapsack DP","subtopicId":"knapsack-dp","difficulty":"hard","title":"Coin Change II vs Combination Sum IV","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Coin Change II (LC 518) counts combinations. Combination Sum IV (LC 377) counts permutations. Which has COINS as outer loop?","answer":"Coin Change II — coins outer prevents duplicate combos; Comb Sum IV has TARGET as outer loop","acceptedAnswers":["Coin Change II","CC2 outer coins","coin change coins outer"],"choices":["Coin Change II — coins outer prevents duplicate combos; Comb Sum IV has TARGET as outer loop","Combination Sum IV — coins outer","Both have coins outer","Neither — both use target outer but different base cases"],"tags":["dp","knapsack","permutations","contrast"],"timeLimitSec":25,"masteryWeight":1.6,"fullCode":"def change(amount,coins):\n    dp=[0]*(amount+1); dp[0]=1\n    for coin in coins:          # COINS outer = combinations\n        for w in range(coin,amount+1): dp[w]+=dp[w-coin]\n    return dp[amount]\n\ndef combinationSum4(nums,target):\n    dp=[0]*(target+1); dp[0]=1\n    for w in range(1,target+1):  # TARGET outer = permutations\n        for num in nums:\n            if w>=num: dp[w]+=dp[w-num]\n    return dp[target]","fullExplanation":"COMBINATIONS = COINS outer: each denomination once per pass → [1,2] and [2,1] same combo. PERMUTATIONS = TARGET outer: try all coins for each amount → different orders counted. Mnemonic: ORDER MATTERS → TARGET outer.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/coin-change-ii/","https://leetcode.com/problems/combination-sum-iv/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-dp-lcs","problemId":"","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Dual-Sequence DP","subtopicId":"dual-sequence","difficulty":"medium","title":"LCS — mismatch formula","questionType":"fill_blank","prompt":"LCS (LC 1143): dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1]+1 if match, else ___.","answer":"max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])","acceptedAnswers":["max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])","max skip either"],"choices":[],"tags":["dp","lcs","transition"],"timeLimitSec":15,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"def longestCommonSubsequence(s1,s2):\n    m,n=len(s1),len(s2)\n    dp=[[0]*(n+1) for _ in range(m+1)]\n    for i in range(1,m+1):\n        for j in range(1,n+1):\n            if s1[i-1]==s2[j-1]: dp[i][j]=dp[i-1][j-1]+1\n            else: dp[i][j]=max(dp[i-1][j],dp[i][j-1])\n    return dp[m][n]","fullExplanation":"LCS mismatch: max(skip s1 char, skip s2 char). Edit Distance mismatch: 1+min(delete,insert,replace). Know both transitions.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-common-subsequence/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-dp-edit","problemId":"","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Dual-Sequence DP","subtopicId":"dual-sequence","difficulty":"medium","title":"Edit Distance — mismatch","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Edit Distance (LC 72): when s1[i]!=s2[j], dp[i][j] = 1 + min(___)?","answer":"1 + min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j-1])  — delete, insert, replace","acceptedAnswers":["min of three","dp[i-1][j] dp[i][j-1] dp[i-1][j-1]"],"choices":["1 + min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1], dp[i-1][j-1])  — delete, insert, replace","1 + max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])","dp[i-1][j-1] + 2","max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) — same as LCS"],"tags":["dp","edit-distance","transition"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.3,"fullCode":"def minDistance(s1,s2):\n    m,n=len(s1),len(s2)\n    dp=[[0]*(n+1) for _ in range(m+1)]\n    for i in range(m+1): dp[i][0]=i\n    for j in range(n+1): dp[0][j]=j\n    for i in range(1,m+1):\n        for j in range(1,n+1):\n            if s1[i-1]==s2[j-1]: dp[i][j]=dp[i-1][j-1]\n            else: dp[i][j]=1+min(dp[i-1][j],dp[i][j-1],dp[i-1][j-1])","fullExplanation":"3 ops: dp[i-1][j]=delete s1[i], dp[i][j-1]=insert s2[j], dp[i-1][j-1]=replace. Contrast with LCS mismatch=max.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/edit-distance/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-dp-lis","problemId":"","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Non-constant Transition DP","subtopicId":"non-constant","difficulty":"hard","title":"LIS — O(n²) vs O(n log n)","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"LIS (LC 300): what can O(n²) DP do that O(n log n) tails CANNOT easily?","answer":"Reconstruct the actual LIS — tails overwrites values, making path reconstruction hard","acceptedAnswers":["reconstruct subsequence","path reconstruction"],"choices":["Reconstruct the actual LIS — tails overwrites values, making path reconstruction hard","Handle duplicate values","Work on 2D input","Run faster — better constants"],"tags":["dp","lis","tradeoff","contrast"],"timeLimitSec":25,"masteryWeight":1.4,"fullCode":"def lisDP(nums):\n    dp=[1]*len(nums)\n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if nums[j]<nums[i]: dp[i]=max(dp[i],dp[j]+1)\n    return max(dp)\n\nimport bisect\ndef lisBin(nums):\n    tails=[]\n    for n in nums:\n        pos=bisect.bisect_left(tails,n)\n        if pos==len(tails): tails.append(n)\n        else: tails[pos]=n  # NOT actual LIS\n    return len(tails)","fullExplanation":"O(n²) DP: dp[i]=LIS ending at i → backtrack for reconstruction. O(n log n) tails: smallest tail for each length → binary search. Tails not actual LIS. For reconstruction: DP. For length only: tails.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-increasing-subsequence/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-gr-interval-sched","problemId":"lc-435-non-overlap","topic":"Greedy","topicId":"greedy","subtopic":"Interval Scheduling","subtopicId":"interval-scheduling","difficulty":"medium","title":"Non-overlapping Intervals — sort key","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Non-overlapping Intervals (LC 435): to minimize removals, sort by ___.","answer":"END TIME — earliest-deadline-first maximizes intervals kept","acceptedAnswers":["end time","sort by end"],"choices":["END TIME — earliest-deadline-first maximizes intervals kept","Start time — chronological order","Length (shortest first)","Start time descending"],"tags":["greedy","intervals","sort-key"],"timeLimitSec":18,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"def eraseOverlapIntervals(intervals):\n    intervals.sort(key=lambda x:x[1])  # END TIME\n    count=0; prev_end=float('-inf')\n    for start,end in intervals:\n        if start>=prev_end: prev_end=end\n        else: count+=1\n    return count","fullExplanation":"Earliest deadline first: sort by end time. Keep intervals that don't overlap with last kept. Overlapping ones are removed. Minimizes removals.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/non-overlapping-intervals/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-ll-cycle","problemId":"lc-141-cycle","topic":"Linked List","topicId":"linked-list","subtopic":"Fast/Slow Pointers","subtopicId":"fast-slow-pointers","difficulty":"easy","title":"Cycle Detection — why they meet","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Floyd's cycle: once inside cycle, why do fast (2x) and slow (1x) always meet?","answer":"Fast gains 1 step per iteration in cycle; gap decreases by 1 each step until 0","acceptedAnswers":["gap decreases by 1","fast gains 1 step"],"choices":["Fast gains 1 step per iteration in cycle; gap decreases by 1 each step until 0","Fast laps slow after cycle_length steps","They meet at cycle entry point exactly","Slow waits; fast loops until returns"],"tags":["linked-list","cycle","proof"],"timeLimitSec":18,"masteryWeight":1.1,"fullCode":"def hasCycle(head):\n    slow=fast=head\n    while fast and fast.next:\n        slow=slow.next; fast=fast.next.next\n        if slow==fast: return True\n    return False","fullExplanation":"Inside cycle: relative speed of fast = +1/iteration. Gap G → after G steps gap=0 → they meet. For cycle start (LC 142): reset one to head, advance both 1 step → meet at cycle start.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/linked-list-cycle/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-ll-reverse","problemId":"lc-206-reverse","topic":"Linked List","topicId":"linked-list","subtopic":"Reversal","subtopicId":"reversal","difficulty":"easy","title":"Reverse Linked List — order","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Reverse Linked List (LC 206) iterative: correct order of pointer updates?","answer":"1. nxt=cur.next  2. cur.next=prev  3. prev=cur  4. cur=nxt","acceptedAnswers":["save next first","nxt cur.next prev cur"],"choices":["1. nxt=cur.next  2. cur.next=prev  3. prev=cur  4. cur=nxt","1. cur.next=prev  2. prev=cur  3. cur=cur.next (no save)","1. prev=cur  2. cur.next=prev  3. cur=cur.next","1. cur=cur.next  2. cur.next=prev  3. prev=cur"],"tags":["linked-list","reversal","pointer-order"],"timeLimitSec":15,"masteryWeight":1.0,"fullCode":"def reverseList(head):\n    prev=None; cur=head\n    while cur:\n        nxt=cur.next    # SAVE\n        cur.next=prev   # REVERSE\n        prev=cur        # advance prev\n        cur=nxt         # advance cur\n    return prev","fullExplanation":"Save cur.next FIRST before overwriting. Common mistake: overwriting cur.next before saving → lose rest of list. End: prev=new head.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/reverse-linked-list/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-trie","problemId":"","topic":"Tries","topicId":"tries","subtopic":"Prefix Matching","subtopicId":"prefix-matching","difficulty":"medium","title":"Trie — when vs hashset","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Need to check if words START WITH a prefix. Trie vs hashset?","answer":"Trie: O(L) prefix natively; hashset: O(N*L) for prefix scan","acceptedAnswers":["trie O(L) prefix","hashset no prefix support"],"choices":["Trie: O(L) prefix natively; hashset: O(N*L) for prefix scan","Trie uses less memory","Trie is faster for exact lookup","Trie allows O(1) deletion"],"tags":["trie","prefix","recognition"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.1,"fullCode":"class TrieNode:\n    def __init__(self): self.ch={}; self.end=False\nclass Trie:\n    def __init__(self): self.root=TrieNode()\n    def insert(self,w):\n        n=self.root\n        for c in w:\n            if c not in n.ch: n.ch[c]=TrieNode()\n            n=n.ch[c]\n        n.end=True\n    def startsWith(self,p):\n        n=self.root\n        for c in p:\n            if c not in n.ch: return False\n            n=n.ch[c]\n        return True","fullExplanation":"Trie: 'prefix', 'starts with', 'autocomplete', 'word search with wildcards'. O(L) insert/search/prefix. Hashset: O(L) exact, O(N*L) prefix.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/implement-trie-prefix-tree/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-seg","problemId":"","topic":"Segment Tree","topicId":"segment-tree","subtopic":"Range Query","subtopicId":"range-query","difficulty":"hard","title":"Segment Tree — when to use","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Range sum QUERIES + point UPDATES, many of each. Best structure?","answer":"Segment Tree — O(log n) both; prefix sum is O(1) query but O(n) update","acceptedAnswers":["segment tree","O(log n) both"],"choices":["Segment Tree — O(log n) both; prefix sum is O(1) query but O(n) update","Prefix sum — O(1) query better overall","Fenwick only — segment tree too complex","HashMap — O(1) for both"],"tags":["segment-tree","range-query","vs-prefix-sum"],"timeLimitSec":20,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"class SegTree:\n    def __init__(self,n,a):\n        self.n=n; self.t=[0]*(2*n)\n        for i in range(n): self.t[n+i]=a[i]\n        for i in range(n-1,0,-1): self.t[i]=self.t[2*i]+self.t[2*i+1]\n    def update(self,i,v):\n        i+=self.n; self.t[i]=v\n        while i>1: i//=2; self.t[i]=self.t[2*i]+self.t[2*i+1]\n    def query(self,l,r):\n        res=0; l+=self.n; r+=self.n\n        while l<r:\n            if l&1: res+=self.t[l]; l+=1\n            if r&1: r-=1; res+=self.t[r]\n            l//=2; r//=2\n        return res","fullExplanation":"Range query+point update → Segment Tree or Fenwick, both O(log n). Prefix sum: O(1) query, O(n) update. Use Seg Tree when you need range min/max (Fenwick doesn't support that).","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-fc-bfs","problemId":"","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"BFS","subtopicId":"bfs","difficulty":"easy","title":"BFS data structure","questionType":"flashcard","prompt":"BFS uses ___ (data structure). DFS uses ___.","answer":"BFS: queue/deque (FIFO). DFS: stack/recursion (LIFO).","acceptedAnswers":["queue","BFS queue DFS stack"],"choices":[],"tags":["bfs","dfs","flashcard"],"timeLimitSec":10,"masteryWeight":0.8,"fullCode":"from collections import deque\nqueue=deque([start]); visited={start}\nwhile queue:\n    node=queue.popleft()\n    for nei in graph[node]:\n        if nei not in visited: visited.add(nei); queue.append(nei)","fullExplanation":"BFS=queue(FIFO): all neighbors before going deeper. DFS=stack: go deep first. BFS guarantees shortest path in unweighted.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-fc-heap","problemId":"","topic":"Heap / Priority Queue","topicId":"heap-priority-queue","subtopic":"Python Heap","subtopicId":"python-heap","difficulty":"easy","title":"Python max heap trick","questionType":"flashcard","prompt":"Python heapq is MIN heap only. How to simulate MAX heap?","answer":"Negate values: push -x, pop and negate (-heapq.heappop(heap))","acceptedAnswers":["negate","push -x"],"choices":[],"tags":["heap","python","flashcard"],"timeLimitSec":10,"masteryWeight":0.8,"fullCode":"import heapq\nh=[]\nheapq.heappush(h,-3); heapq.heappush(h,-1)\nprint(-heapq.heappop(h))  # 3","fullExplanation":"Python heapq: min heap. Max heap: push -x, pop and negate. For tuples: (-priority, value).","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-fc-backtrack","problemId":"","topic":"Backtracking","topicId":"backtracking","subtopic":"Template","subtopicId":"template","difficulty":"easy","title":"Backtracking 3 steps","questionType":"flashcard","prompt":"The 3 steps of backtracking: Choose → ___ → ___.","answer":"Choose → Explore → Unchoose (undo the choice)","acceptedAnswers":["explore unchoose","explore / unchoose"],"choices":[],"tags":["backtracking","template","flashcard"],"timeLimitSec":10,"masteryWeight":0.8,"fullCode":"def bt(path,choices):\n    if base_case(path): result.append(path[:]); return\n    for c in choices:\n        path.append(c)         # CHOOSE\n        bt(path,next_choices)  # EXPLORE\n        path.pop()             # UNCHOOSE","fullExplanation":"Always undo choice after recursive call. Reuse the same path list. Recursive call explores all paths starting with this choice.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-fc-visited","problemId":"","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"BFS","subtopicId":"bfs","difficulty":"easy","title":"BFS — when to mark visited","questionType":"flashcard","prompt":"In BFS, mark node as visited when you ___ it (not when you process it). Why?","answer":"When you ENQUEUE it — prevents duplicate queue entries and exponential work","acceptedAnswers":["enqueue","push","before queue.append"],"choices":[],"tags":["bfs","visited","pitfall","flashcard"],"timeLimitSec":12,"masteryWeight":1.0,"fullCode":"q=deque([start]); vis={start}  # mark at push\nwhile q:\n    node=q.popleft()\n    for nei in neighbors(node):\n        if nei not in vis:\n            vis.add(nei)  # BEFORE push\n            q.append(nei)","fullExplanation":"Mark BEFORE pushing. If you mark at pop, same node pushed multiple times → exponential work in dense graphs.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-fc-memo-tab","problemId":"","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Memoization","subtopicId":"memoization","difficulty":"easy","title":"Memoization vs Tabulation","questionType":"flashcard","prompt":"Which avoids stack overflow for deep recursion: memoization or tabulation?","answer":"Tabulation (bottom-up) — iterative, no stack overflow risk","acceptedAnswers":["tabulation","bottom-up","iterative"],"choices":[],"tags":["dp","flashcard"],"timeLimitSec":10,"masteryWeight":0.8,"fullCode":"@lru_cache(None)\ndef fib(n):  # memoization — recursion depth risk\n    if n<=1: return n\n    return fib(n-1)+fib(n-2)\n\ndef fib_tab(n):  # tabulation — safe\n    dp=[0]*(n+1); dp[1]=1\n    for i in range(2,n+1): dp[i]=dp[i-1]+dp[i-2]\n    return dp[n]","fullExplanation":"Memoization: @lru_cache, risk at Python limit ~1000. Tabulation: iterative, handles n=10^6+. For interviews: memoization faster to write; tabulation required for deep recursion.","links":[],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-gr-rotting","problemId":"lc-994-rotting","topic":"Graphs","topicId":"graphs","subtopic":"BFS Multi-source","subtopicId":"bfs-multi-source","difficulty":"medium","title":"Rotting Oranges — multi-source BFS","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Rotting Oranges (LC 994): all rotten oranges spread simultaneously. Why multi-source BFS?","answer":"Init queue with ALL rotten oranges at t=0 — they all spread simultaneously, not sequentially","acceptedAnswers":["multi-source","all rotten at t=0","all rotten oranges"],"choices":["Init queue with ALL rotten oranges at t=0 — they all spread simultaneously, not sequentially","Run separate BFS from each rotten orange, take minimum","DFS from each rotten orange to find farthest fresh","Sort rotten oranges, process left to right"],"tags":["graphs","bfs","multi-source","matrix"],"timeLimitSec":18,"masteryWeight":1.2,"fullCode":"from collections import deque\ndef orangesRotting(grid):\n    m,n=len(grid),len(grid[0]); q=deque(); fresh=0\n    for r in range(m):\n        for c in range(n):\n            if grid[r][c]==2: q.append((r,c,0))\n            elif grid[r][c]==1: fresh+=1\n    mins=0\n    while q:\n        r,c,t=q.popleft()\n        for dr,dc in [(0,1),(0,-1),(1,0),(-1,0)]:\n            nr,nc=r+dr,c+dc\n            if 0<=nr<m and 0<=nc<n and grid[nr][nc]==1:\n                grid[nr][nc]=2; fresh-=1; q.append((nr,nc,t+1)); mins=t+1\n    return mins if fresh==0 else -1","fullExplanation":"Multi-source BFS: init ALL rotten oranges at t=0. Each BFS step = 1 minute, all spread simultaneously. If fresh>0 after BFS → return -1.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/rotting-oranges/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
    {"id":"hq-dp-burst","problemId":"lc-312-burst-balloons","topic":"Dynamic Programming","topicId":"dynamic-programming","subtopic":"Interval DP","subtopicId":"interval-dp","difficulty":"hard","title":"Burst Balloons — last burst insight","questionType":"multiple_choice","prompt":"Burst Balloons (LC 312): dp[i][j]=max coins bursting (i..j). Think about the ___ balloon burst in the range.","answer":"LAST — when k is last in (i,j), neighbors always i and j → subproblems [i,k] and [k,j] are independent","acceptedAnswers":["last","last balloon","last burst"],"choices":["LAST — when k is last in (i,j), neighbors always i and j → subproblems [i,k] and [k,j] are independent","FIRST — simplifies range","MIDDLE — divide and conquer","MINIMUM — greedy choice"],"tags":["dp","interval-dp","key-insight"],"timeLimitSec":25,"masteryWeight":1.5,"fullCode":"def maxCoins(nums):\n    nums=[1]+nums+[1]; n=len(nums)\n    dp=[[0]*n for _ in range(n)]\n    for l in range(2,n):\n        for i in range(0,n-l):\n            j=i+l\n            for k in range(i+1,j):  # k = LAST burst\n                dp[i][j]=max(dp[i][j],dp[i][k]+nums[i]*nums[k]*nums[j]+dp[k][j])\n    return dp[0][n-1]","fullExplanation":"Think LAST burst. If k is last in (i,j), only neighbors are i and j → coins=nums[i]*nums[k]*nums[j]. Left [i,k] and right [k,j] don't interact → DP works.","links":["https://leetcode.com/problems/burst-balloons/"],"revealFullCodeAfterAnswer":True,"revealFullExplanationAfterAnswer":True},
